          %p
            After a long hiatus, I’d like to renew the discussion of axiomatic categorical set theory, more specifically the Elementary Theory of the Category of Sets (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"}> ETCS
            ).
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/10/etcs-internalizing-the-logic"} Last time
            I blogged about this, I made some initial forays into “internalizing logic” in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %em colimits
            in a category satisfying the ETCS axioms (an
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
                category
          %p
            (If you’re just joining us now, and you already know some of the jargon, an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
              category
            is a well-pointed topos that satisfies the axiom of choice and with a natural numbers object. We are trying to build up some of the elementary theory of such categories from scratch, with a view toward foundations of mathematics.)
          %p But let’s see — where were we? Since it’s been a while, I was tempted to review the philosophy behind this undertaking (why one would go to all the trouble of setting up a categories-based alternative to ZFC, when time-tested ZFC is able to express virtually all of present-day mathematics on the basis of a reasonably short list of axioms?). But in the interest of time and space, I’ll confine myself to a few remarks.
          %p
            As we said, a chief difference between ZFC and ETCS resides in how ETCS treats the issue of membership. In ZFC, membership is a global binary relation: we can take any two “sets”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
            and ask whether
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in B
            = succeed "," do
              %em e.g.
          %p
            Further, and far more radical: in ETCS the membership relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in A
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %em function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            “belongs” to only one set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            at a time. We can think of this as “declaring” how we are thinking of an element, that is, declaring which set (or which type) an element is being considered as belonging to. (In the jargon, ETCS is a
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em typed theory
            %em qua
            rational number or
            %em qua
            complex number,
            = succeed ")." do
              %em etc.
          %p
            I believe it is reasonable to grant this principle a foundational status, but: rigorous adherence to this principle completely changes the face of what set theory looks like. If elements “belong” only to one set at a time, how then do we even
            %em define
            such basic concepts as subsets and intersections? These are some of the issues we discussed last time.
          %p
            There are other significant differences between ZFC and ETCS: stylistically, or in terms of presentation, ZFC is more “top-down” and ETCS is more “bottom-up”. For example, in ZFC, one can pretty much define a subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \in X : P \}
            by writing down a first-order formula
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            in the language; the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comprehension"} comprehension
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+separation"} separation
            %em derives
            a comprehension scheme as one manually constructs the internal logic, in stages, using the simple tools of adjunctions and universal properties. We started doing some of that in our last post. So: with ZFC it’s more as if you can just hop in the car and go; with ETCS you build the car engine from smaller parts with your bare hands, but in the process you become an expert mechanic, and are not so rigidly attached to a particular make and model (
            = succeed "," do
              %em e.g.
          %p But, in all fairness, that is perhaps the biggest obstacle to learning ETCS: at the outset, the tools available [mainly, the idea of a universal property] are quite simple but parsimonious, and one has to learn how to build some set-theoretic and logical concepts normally taken as “obvious” from the ground up. (Talk about “foundations”!) On the plus side, by building big logical machines from scratch, one gains a great deal of insight into the inner workings of logic, with a corresponding gain in precision and control and modularity when one would like to use these developments to design, say, automated deduction systems (where there tend to be strong advantages to using type-theoretic frameworks).
          %dl
            %dt
              Enough philosophy for now; readers may refer to my
              %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/01/zfc-and-etcs-elementary-theory-of-the-category-of-sets"} earlier
              %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/10/etcs-internalizing-the-logic/"} posts
              for more. Let’s get to work, shall we? Our last post was about the structure of (and relationships between) posets of subobjects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(X)
              %em relative
              to objects
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %em absolute
              constructions, in particular finite coproducts and coequalizers. In this post we will focus on coproducts.
            %dd
              %p
                %strong Note to the experts.
                Most textbook treatments of the formal development of topos theory (as for example Mac Lane–Moerdijk) are efficient but highly technical, involving for instance the slice theorem for toposes and, in the construction of colimits, recourse to Beck’s theorem in monad theory applied to the double power-set monad [following the elegant construction of Par]. The very abstract nature of this style of argumentation (which in the application of Beck’s theorem expresses ideas of fourth-order set theory and higher) is no doubt partly responsible for the somewhat fearsome reputation of topos theory.
            %dd
              %p In these notes I take a much less efficient but much more elementary approach, based on an arrangement of ideas which I hope can be seen as “natural” from the point of view of naive set theory. I learned of this approach from Myles Tierney, who was my PhD supervisor, and who with Bill Lawvere co-founded elementary topos theory, but I am not aware of any place where the details of this approach have been written up before now. I should also mention that the approach taken here is not as “purist” as many topos theorists might want; for example, here and there I take advantage of the strong extensionality axiom of ETCS to simplify some arguments.
          %h3#the_empty_set_and_twovalued_logic The Empty Set and Two-Valued Logic
          %p
            We begin with the easy observation that a terminal category,
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            with just one object and one morphism (the identity), satisfies all the ETCS axioms. Ditto for any category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            (where every object is terminal). Such boring ETCS categories are called
            = succeed ";" do
              %em degenerate
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            be an ETCS category (see
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/01/zfc-and-etcs-elementary-theory-of-the-category-of-sets"} here
            for the ETCS axioms). Objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            are generally called “sets”, and morphisms are generally called “functions” or “maps”.
          %p
            %strong Proposition 0.
            If an ETCS category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            is a preorder, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            is degenerate.
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            Recall that a preorder is a category in which there is at most one morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
            for any two objects
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to 1
            to any terminal set defines a subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq 1
            distinct from the subset defined by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A, t : 1 \to P(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            are terminal.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            Assume from now on that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            is a nondegenerate ETCS category.
          %p
            %strong Proposition 1.
            There are at least two
            = succeed "," do
              %em truth values
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to P(1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            By proposition 0, there exist sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y
            and two distinct functions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g : X \to Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : 1 \to X
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi g
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f x \neq g x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to 1
            of the pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f x, g x : 1 \to Y
            is then a proper subset of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_E, \chi_1 : 1 \to P(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            %strong Proposition 2.
            There are at most two truth values
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to P(1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U, V \subseteq 1
            are distinct subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \neq U \cap V
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \neq U \cap V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_U : U \subseteq U
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \cap V \subseteq U
            are distinct subsets, with distinct classifying maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mi U
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi V
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_{1_U}, \chi_{U \cap V} : U \to P(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : 1 \to U
            distinguishing these classifying maps. Because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is terminal, we then infer
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \subseteq U
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U = 1
            as subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            can be a proper subset of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            By Propositions 1 and 2, there is a unique proper subset of the terminal object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to 1
            denote this subset. Its domain may be called an “empty set”; by the preceding proposition, it has no proper subsets. The classifying map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to P1
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \subseteq 1
            is the truth value we call “false”.
          %p
            %strong Proposition 3.
            0 is an initial object,
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            there exists a unique function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to X
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            Uniqueness: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g : 0 \to X
            are maps, then their equalizer
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : E \to 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = g
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi t
                          %mi X
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle 0 \to 1 \stackrel{t_X}{\to} P X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi σ
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle X \stackrel{\sigma}{\to} P X
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_X
            is “global truth” (classifying the subset
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \subseteq X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            is the “singleton mapping
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto \{ x \}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta : X \subseteq X \times X
            (last time we saw
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            is monic). Take their pullback. The component of the pullback parallel to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            is a mono
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to 0
            which again is an isomorphism, whence we get a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \cong P \to X
            using the other component of the pullback.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            %strong Remark.
            For the “purists”, an alternative construction of the initial set 0 that avoids use of the strong extensionality axiom is to define the subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \subseteq 1
            to be “the intersection all subsets of
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[\phi\right] \subseteq 1
            of the map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi t
                          %mrow
                            %mi P
                            %mn 1
                    %mi PP
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \phi = (1 \stackrel{t_{P1}}{\to} PP1 \stackrel{\bigcap}{\to} P1);
          %p
            where the first arrow represents the class of all subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \subseteq 1
            has no proper subsets, and then the proof of proposition 3 carries over verbatim.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            %strong Corollary 1.
            For any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X
            is initial.
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            By
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/10/etcs-internalizing-the-logic"} cartesian closure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X \to Y
            are in bijection with maps of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to Y^X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            %strong Corollary 2.
            If there exists
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is initial.
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            The composite of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle f, 1_X \rangle : X \to 0 \times X
            followed by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2 : 0 \times X \to X
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2
            followed by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle f, 1_X \rangle : X \to 0 \times X
            is also an identity since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X
            is initial by Corollary 1. Hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is isomorphic to an initial object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            By Corollary 2, for any object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            the arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to Y
            is vacuously monic, hence defines a subset.
          %p
            %strong Proposition 4.
            If
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ≅
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \not\cong 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : 1 \to X
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            Under the assumption,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has at least two distinct subsets:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \subseteq X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_X : X \subseteq X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_0, \chi_1 : X \to P1
            are distinguished by some element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : 1 \to X
          %h3#external_unions_and_internal_joins External Unions and Internal Joins
          %p
            One of the major goals in this post is to construct finite coproducts in an ETCS category. As in ordinary set theory, we will construct these as disjoint unions. This means we need to discuss unions first; as should be expected by now, in ETCS unions are considered locally,
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            = succeed "." do
              %em subsets of a given set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B \subseteq X
            be subsets.
          %p
            To define the union
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup B \subseteq X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/10/etcs-internalizing-the-logic/"} last time
            ),
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \bigcap : P P X \to P X,
          %dl
            %dt
              to the element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to P P X
              that represents the set of all subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              containing
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to P X
              represents
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to P P X
              corresponds to the intersection of two subsets
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                      %mo ∩
                      %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ C \in P X : A \subseteq C \} \:\cap\: \{ C \in P X : B \subseteq C \} \subseteq P X
            %dd
              %p
                %strong Remark.
                Remember that in ETCS we are using
                %em generalized
                elements:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \in P X
                really means a function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C : U \to P X
                over some domain
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo [
                          %mi C
                          %mo ]
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi U
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[C\right] \subseteq U \times X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                here is a subset
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
                = succeed "\u201D?" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi χ
                        %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A : 1 \to P X
                over to the domain
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                (that is, we form the composite
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mi U
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo !
                          %mn 1
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi χ
                                %mi A
                          %mi P
                          %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle U \stackrel{!}{\to} 1 \stackrel{\chi_A}{\to} P X
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo :
                        %mi U
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C : U \to P X
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mi A
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A \leq C
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ×
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mrow
                          %mo [
                          %mi C
                          %mo ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \times A \subseteq \left[C\right]
          %p
            We need to construct the subsets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ C \in P X : A \subseteq C \}, \{ C \in P X : B \subseteq C \}
            %em derive
            a comprehension scheme!) However, one way of defining subsets in ETCS is by taking loci of equations; here, we express the condition
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mi C
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq \left[C\right]
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi A
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A \leq C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi A
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\chi_A \Rightarrow C) = t_X
          %p
            where the right side is the predicate “true over
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Thus we construct the subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{C \in P X: A \subseteq C\}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            via the pullback:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi C
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "1"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi t
                                  %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi χ
                                %mi A
                              %mo ⇒
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{matrix}
                  \\{ C : A \le C \} &amp; \longrightarrow &amp; 1 \\
                  \\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{\scriptsize{t_X}} \\
                  P X &amp; \underset{\chi_A \Rightarrow -}{\longrightarrow} &amp; P X
                  \\end{matrix}
          %p
            Let me take a moment to examine what this diagram means exactly.
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/10/etcs-internalizing-the-logic"} Last time
            we constructed an internal implication operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow : P1 \times P1 \to P1
          %p and now, in the pullback diagram above, what we are implicitly doing is lifting this to an operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi PX
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow_X : P X \times PX \to P X
          %p
            The easy and cheap way of doing this is to remember the isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi P
                  %msup
                    %mn 1
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X \cong P1^X
            we used last time to uncover the cartesian closed structure, and apply this to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mi P
                    %msup
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %msup
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi X
                    %mi P
                    %msup
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle P1^X \times P1^X \cong (P1 \times P1)^X \stackrel{\Rightarrow^X}{\to} P1^X
          %p
            to define
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow_X : P X \times P X \to P X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times P X \times P X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ (x, T, S) \in X \times P X \times P X : x \in_X T \Rightarrow x \in_X S \}
          %p
            %strong Remark.
            Similarly we can define a meet operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge_X : P X \times P X \to P X
            by exponentiating the internal meet
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P1 \times P 1 \to P 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P1
            lift to the corresponding identities for the operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge_X, \Rightarrow_X
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^X
          %p
            Hence, for the fixed subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
            (classified by
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A : 1 \to P X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi A
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A \Rightarrow - : P X \to P X
          %p classifies the subset
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ (x, S) : x \in_X A \Rightarrow x \in_X S \} \hookrightarrow X \times P X
          %p
            Finally, in the pullback diagram above, we are pulling back the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi A
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A \Rightarrow -
            against
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_X
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/10/etcs-internalizing-the-logic"} last time
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \subseteq X \times Y
          %p
            we defined
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi Y
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_Y R \subseteq X
            to be the pullback of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi PY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_Y : 1 \hookrightarrow PY
            along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi PY
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_R : X \to PY
          %p Putting all this together, the pullback diagram above expresses the definition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ C \in P X : A \subseteq C \} := \{ C \in P X : \forall_{x \in X} \: x \in_X A \Rightarrow x \in_X C \}
          %p that one would expect “naively”.
          %p
            Now that all the relevant constructions are in place, we show that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup B
            is the join of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            in the poset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(X)
            %em constructing
            the internal logic, we will have to hunker down and prove some logic things normally taken for granted or zipped through without much thought. For example, the internal intersection operator was defined with the help of internal universal quantification, and we will need to establish some formal properties of that.
          %p
            Here is a useful general principle for doing internal logic calculations. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi PY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_R : X \to PY
            be the classifying map of a subset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \subseteq X \times Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : U \to X
            be a function. Then the composite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi R
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi PY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_R f : U \to PY
            classifies the subset
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi Y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f \times 1_Y)^{-1}(R) \subseteq U \times Y
          %p
            so that one has the general identity
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi R
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo ×
                        %mn 1
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_R f = \chi_{(f \times 1)^{-1}(R)}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi PY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to PY
            into a form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi R
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_R f
            which one can more easily recognize. For lack of a better term, I’ll call this the “pullback principle”.
          %p
            %strong Lemma 1.
            Given a relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \hookrightarrow X \times Y
            and a constant
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c : 1 \to Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi Y
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi c
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_Y R \subseteq (1_X \times c)^{-1}(R)
          %p
            as subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c : 1 \to Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi Y
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi implies
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_{y : Y} R(x, y) \quad implies \quad R(x, c)
          %p
            as predicates over elements
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            As we recalled above,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi Y
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_Y R \subseteq X
            was defined to be
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi χ
                      %mi R
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_R^{-1}(t_Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi PY
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_Y : 1 \to PY
            along the classifying map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi PY
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_R : X \to PY
          %p Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Pc
                  %mo :
                  %mi PY
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pc : PY \to P1
          %p
            be the map which classifies the subset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi PY
                    %mo :
                    %mi c
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ S \in PY : c \in_Y S\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %msup
                    %mn 1
                    %mi c
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %msup
                    %mn 1
                    %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %msup
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P1^c: P1^Y \to P1^1
          %p
            under the canonical isomorphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PY
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi P
                    %msup
                      %mn 1
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PY \cong P1^Y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %msup
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P1^1 \cong P1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \mapsto f(c)
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in Y
            into an element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                    %msup
                      %mn 1
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in P1^Y
          %p
            Using the adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (- \times Y) \dashv (-)^Y
            of cartesian closure, the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mi R
                    %mi PY
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi Pc
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle X \stackrel{\chi_R}{\to} PY \stackrel{Pc}{\to} P1
          %p transforms to the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi c
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi χ
                        %mi R
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times 1 \stackrel{1_X \times c}{\to} X \times Y \stackrel{\chi_{R}}{\to} P1
          %p
            so by the pullback principle,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Pc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Pc)\chi_R : X \times 1 \to P1
            classifies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi c
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1_X \times c)^{-1}(R) \subseteq X \times 1 \cong X
          %p Equivalently,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi c
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi χ
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Pc
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1_X \times c)^{-1}(R) = \chi_{R}^{-1} (Pc)^{-1}(t) \qquad (1)
          %p
            Also, as subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PY
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PY
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi PY
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Pc
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t_Y : 1 \hookrightarrow PY) \subseteq (Pc)^{-1}(t) \qquad (2)
          %p
            [this just says that  belongs to the subset classified by , or equivalently that  is in the subset ]. Applying the pullback operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi χ
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_{R}^{-1}
            to (2), and comparing to (1), Lemma 1 follows.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            %strong Lemma 2.
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \subseteq S
            as subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times Y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi Y
                    %mi R
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi Y
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_Y R \subseteq \forall_Y S
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            From the last post, we have an adjunction:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi if
                  %mi and
                  %mi only
                  %mi if
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi Y
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \times Y \subseteq S \quad if and only if \quad T \subseteq \forall_Y S
          %p
            for any subset of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \subseteq X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi Y
                    %mi R
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_Y R \times Y \subseteq S
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi Y
                  %mi R
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_Y R \times Y \subseteq R \subseteq S
          %p
            where the first inclusion follows from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi Y
                    %mi R
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi Y
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_Y R \subseteq \forall_Y R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            Next, recall from the
            %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/10/etcs-internalizing-the-logic"} last post
            that the internal intersection of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : 1 \to P P X
            was defined by interpreting the following formula on the right:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \bigcap F = \forall_{S \in P X} (S \in_{P X} F) \Rightarrow (x \in_X S)
          %p
            %strong Lemma 3.
            If
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \leq G : 1 \to P P X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mi G
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \bigcap G \leq \bigcap F : 1 \to P X
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : 1 \to P P X
            classifies the subset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ S \in P X : S \in_{P X} F \}
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is identified with the predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \in_{P X} F
            in the argument
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S \in_{P X} F) \leq (S \in_{P X} G)
            as predicates on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Rightarrow b
            is contravariant in the argument
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            [see the following remark], so
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ≤
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left[(S \in G) \Rightarrow (x \in S)\right] \leq \left[(S \in F) \Rightarrow (x \in S)\right]
          %dl
            %dt
              Now apply Lemma 2 to complete the proof.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            %dd
              %p
                %strong Remark.
                The contravariance of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \Rightarrow b
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
                implies
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (y \Rightarrow b) \leq (x \Rightarrow b)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi c
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \wedge c \leq b
                if and only if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \leq (a \Rightarrow b).
            %dd
              %p Indeed, we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge (y \Rightarrow b) \leq y \wedge (y \Rightarrow b) \leq b \qquad (*)
          %p
            where the first inequality follows from the hypothesis
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi b
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \Rightarrow b \leq y \Rightarrow b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (*)
            implies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (y \Rightarrow b) \leq (x \Rightarrow b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            %strong Theorem 1.
            For subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup B
            is an upper bound of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B \subseteq A \cup B
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            It suffices to prove that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle A = \bigcap \{C \in P X : A \subseteq C\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mo ∩
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ C \in P X : A \subseteq C \} \:\cap\: \{ C \in P X : B \subseteq C \} \subseteq \{ C \in P X : A \subseteq C \}
          %p
            to infer
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq A \cup B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subseteq A \cup B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle A \subseteq \bigcap \{ C \in P X : A \subseteq C \}
          %p The condition we want,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq \{ x \in X : \forall_{S \in P X} (A \subseteq S) \Rightarrow (x \in_X S) \},
          %p
            is, by the adjunction
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (- \times P X) \dashv \forall_{P X} : Sub(X \times P X) \to Sub(X)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times P X \subseteq \{ (x, S) : A \subseteq S \Rightarrow (x \in_X S) \}
          %p
            which, by a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            adjunction, is equivalent to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A \times P X) \cap (X \times \{ S \in P X : A \subseteq S \}) \subseteq \: \in_X \qquad (1)
          %p
            as subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times P X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ S \in P X : A \subseteq S \} = \{ S \in P X : \forall_{x \in X} x \in_X A \Rightarrow x \in_X S \}
          %p
            Using the adjunction
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X \times -) \dashv \forall_X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times \{ S \in P X : \forall_{x \in X} x \in_X A \Rightarrow x \in_X S \}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \subseteq \{ (x, S) : x \in_X A \Rightarrow x \in_X S \} := (A \times P X) \Rightarrow \in_X,
          %p which shows that the left side of (1) is contained in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A \times P X) \cap ((A \times P X) \Rightarrow \in_X) \subseteq \: \in_X,
          %p
            where the last inclusion uses another
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            adjunction. Thus we have established (1) and therefore also the inclusion
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle A \subseteq \bigcap \{S \in P X : A \subseteq S\}
          %p Now we prove the opposite inclusion
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \bigcap \{ S \in P X : A \subseteq S \} \subseteq A,
          %p that is to say
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \in X : \forall_{S \in P X} A \subseteq S \Rightarrow x \in_X S \} \subseteq A \qquad (**)
          %p
            Here we just use Lemma 1, applied to the particular element
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_A : 1 \to P X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (**)
            is contained in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi A
                    %mo ]
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \in X : A \subseteq \left[\chi_A\right] \Rightarrow x \in_X A\}
          %p
            which collapses to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \in X : x \in_X A \} = A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %msub
                        %mi χ
                        %mi A
                      %mo ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \left[\chi_A\right]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            %strong Theorem 2.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup B
            is the least upper bound of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \subseteq X
            is a subset containing both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subseteq X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup B \subseteq C
          %p
            %strong Proof.
            We are required to show that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "mediummathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi C
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \in X : \forall_{S \in P X} \: (A \subseteq S \wedge B \subseteq S) \Rightarrow x \in_X S \} \subseteq C.
          %p
            Again, we just apply Lemma 1 to the particular element
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_C : 1 \to P X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \in X : (A \subseteq C \wedge B \subseteq C) \Rightarrow x \in_X C \}
          %p
            but since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A \subseteq C \wedge B \subseteq C)
            is true by hypothesis (is globally true as a predicate on the implicit variable
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi X
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \in X : t_X \Rightarrow x \in_X C \} = \{ x \in X : x \in_X C \} = C
          %p
            which completes the proof.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            Theorems 1 and 2 show that for any set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(X)
            admits joins. One may go on to show (just on the basis of the topos axioms) that as in the case of meets, the global external operation of taking joins is natural in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee : P1 \times P1 \to P1,
          %p namely, the map which classifies the union of the subsets
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ×
                        %mi t
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \left[\pi_1\right] = P1 \times 1 \stackrel{1 \times t}{\hookrightarrow} P1 \times P1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo [
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ]
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo ×
                        %mn 1
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \left[\pi_2\right] = 1 \times P1 \stackrel{t \times 1}{\hookrightarrow} P1 \times P1
          %p
            and this operation satisfies all the expected identities. In short,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P 1
            carries an internal Heyting algebra structure, as does
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi P
                  %msup
                    %mn 1
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X \cong P 1^X
            for any set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            We will come back to this point later, when we show (as a consequence of strong extensionality) that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P1
            is actually an internal Boolean algebra.
          %h3#construction_of_coproducts Construction of Coproducts
          %p
            Next, we construct coproducts just as we do in ordinary set theory: as disjoint unions. Letting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y
            be sets (objects in an ETCS category), a
            %em disjoint union
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a pair of monos
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Z
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi j
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : X \hookrightarrow Z \qquad j : Y \hookrightarrow Z
          %p
            whose intersection is empty, and whose union or join in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(Z)
            is all of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X + Y
            for a disjoint union.
          %p
            %strong Theorem 3.
            A disjoint union of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            exists.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              It’s enough to embed
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y
              disjointly into
              %em some
              set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(C)
              would then be the requisite
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X+Y
              exists, then there’s a canonical isomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(X + Y) \cong P X \times P Y
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : X + Y \to P(X + Y) \cong P X \times P Y
            %p
              is monic, one thus expects to be able to embed
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              disjointly into
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X \times P Y
            %p
              In detail, define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_X : X \to P X \times P Y
              to be
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mi X
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 1
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mi X
                          %mo ×
                          %msub
                            %mi χ
                            %mn 0
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle X \cong X \times 1 \stackrel{\sigma_X \times \chi_0}{\to} P X \times P Y
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_X
              is the singleton mapping and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_0
              classifies
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \hookrightarrow Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi Y
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_Y :Y \to P X \times P Y
              to be
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ≅
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi χ
                            %mn 0
                          %mo ×
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mi Y
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                      %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle Y \cong 1 \times Y \stackrel{\chi_0 \times \sigma_Y}{\to} P X \times P Y.
            %p
              Clearly
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_Y
              are monic, so to show disjointness we just have to show that their pullback is empty. But their pullback is isomorphic to the cartesian product of the pullbacks of the diagrams
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mi X
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi χ
                            %mn 0
                      %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mn 1
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi χ
                            %mn 0
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mi Y
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle X \stackrel{\sigma_X}{\to} P X \stackrel{\chi_0}{\leftarrow} 1 \qquad 1 \stackrel{\chi_0}{\to} P Y \stackrel{\sigma_Y}{\leftarrow} Y
            %p so it would be enough to show that each (or just one) of these two pullbacks is empty, let’s say the first.
            %p
              Suppose given a map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: A \to X
              which makes the square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:rowalign => "center axis center"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} A
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "1"}
                                %mi h
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "1"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi χ
                                    %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi σ
                                  %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\arrayopts{\rowalign{center axis center}}
                    \\A &amp; \longrightarrow &amp; 1 \\
                    \\mathllap{\scriptsize{h}} \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{\scriptsize{\chi_0}} \\
                    X &amp; \underset{\dlap{\sigma_X}}{\longrightarrow} &amp; P X
                    }
            %p
              commute. Using the pullback principle, the map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi χ
                          %mn 0
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to 1 \stackrel{\chi_0}{\to} P X
              classifies
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times A \hookrightarrow X \times A
            %p
              which is just the empty subset. This must be the same subset as classified by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi X
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi δ
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_X h = \chi_{\delta} h
              (where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta : X \hookrightarrow X \times X
              is the diagonal), which by the pullback principle is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi h
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1_X \times h)^{-1}(\delta) \hookrightarrow X \times A.
            %p An elementary calculation shows this to be the equalizer of the pair of maps
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1, h\pi_2 : X \times A \stackrel{\to}{\to} X.
            %p
              So this equalizer
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is empty. But notice that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi h
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle h, 1 \rangle : A \to X \times A
              equalizes this pair of maps. Therefore we have a map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi E
                      %mo ≅
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to E \cong 0
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≅
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cong 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is the pullback, so the pullback is empty, as was to be shown.
          %p
            %strong Theorem 4.
            Any two disjoint unions of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y
            are canonically isomorphic.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ←
                    %mi Y
                    %mo :
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : X \rightarrow Z \leftarrow Y : j
              is a disjoint union. Define a map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi = \langle \phi_1, \phi_2 \rangle : Z \to P X \times P Y
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1 : Z \to P X
              classifies the subset
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle 1_X, i \rangle : X \to X \times Z
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_2 : Z \to P Y
              classifies the subset
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi Y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle 1_Y, j \rangle : Y \to Y \times Z
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1 i : X \to P X
              classifies
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1_X \times i)^{-1}(\langle 1_X, i \rangle) \hookrightarrow X \times X
            %p
              which is easily seen to be the diagonal on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 1
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1 i = \sigma_X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1 j : Y \to P X
              classifies the subset
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi j
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1_X \times j)^{-1}(\langle 1_X, i \rangle) \hookrightarrow X \times Y
            %p
              which is empty because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              are disjoint embeddings, so
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 1
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi χ
                        %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1 j = \chi_0 : Y \to P X
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mi j
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mi Y
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_2 i = \chi_0 : X \to P Y \qquad \phi_2 j = \sigma_Y : Y \to P Y.
            %p
              Putting all this together, we conclude that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi i = i_X : X \to P X \times P Y
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mi Y
                      %mo :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi j = i_Y : Y \to P X \times P Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_Y
              were defined in the proof of Theorem 3.
            %p
              Next, we show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              is monic. If not, then by strong extensionality, there exist distinct elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c, d : 1 \to Z
              for which
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(c) = \phi(d)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mi d
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1 c = \phi_1 d : 1 \to P X
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 2
                      %mi c
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 2
                      %mi d
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi PY
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_2 c = \phi_2 d : 1 \to PY
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi d
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi d
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mn 1
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^{-1}(i) = d^{-1}(i) \hookrightarrow 1 \qquad c^{-1}(j) = d^{-1}(j) \hookrightarrow 1.
            %p
              If
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi c
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^{-1}(i) = 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c, d : 1 \to Z
              factor through the mono
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : X \to Z
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi i = i_X
              is monic, this would imply that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi (c) \neq \phi (d)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi c
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^{-1}(i) = c \cap i = 0
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \cap j = 0
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi c
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mi j
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \subseteq \neg c \qquad j \subseteq \neg c
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg = (- \Rightarrow 0) : Sub(Z) \to Sub(Z)
              is the negation operator. But then
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi j
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \cup j \subseteq \neg c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 : Z \hookrightarrow Z
              is the union
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \cup j
              by assumption,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg c
              must be the top element
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊤
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Sub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \in Sub(Z)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c : 1 \to Z
              is the bottom element 0. This contradicts the assumption that the topos is nondegenerate. Thus we have shown that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              must be monic.
            %p
              The argument above shows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : Z \hookrightarrow P X \times P Y
              is an upper bound of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_X : X \to P X \times P Y
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi Y
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_Y : Y \to P X \times P Y
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(P X \times P Y)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X + Y
              constructed in Theorem 3 is contained in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo :
                      %mi Z
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : Z \to P X \times P Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(Z)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              is their join in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(Z)
              by assumption of being a disjoint union, so the containment
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X + Y \subseteq Z
              must be an equality. The proof is now complete.
          %p
            %strong Theorem 5.
            The inclusions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_X : X \to X + Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_Y : Y \to X + Y
            exhibit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X + Y
            as the coproduct of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Let
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : Y \to B
              be given functions. Then we have monos
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle 1_X, f \rangle : X \hookrightarrow X \times B \qquad \langle 1_Y, g \rangle : Y \hookrightarrow Y \times B \qquad (1)
            %p
              Now the operation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \times B : Sub(C) \to Sub(C \times B)
              certainly preserves finite meets, and also preserves finite joins because it is left adjoint to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mi B
                      %mo :
                      %mi Sub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Sub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_B : Sub(C \times B) \to Sub(C)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mi Y
                        %mo ×
                        %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times B \stackrel{i_X \times B}{\to} (X + Y) \times B \qquad Y \times B \stackrel{i_Y \times B}{\to} (X + Y) \times B \qquad (2)
            %p
              exhibit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X + Y) \times B
              as a disjoint union of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times B, Y \times B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X + Y) \times B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X + Y
              is isomorphic to the join of these embeddings; this means we have an inclusion
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ↪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle 1, h \rangle : X + Y \hookrightarrow (X + Y) \times B
            %p
              whose restriction to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              yields
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle i_X, f \rangle
              and whose restriction to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              yields
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mi Y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle i_Y, g \rangle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h : X + Y \to B
              extends
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo ,
                      %mi h
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h, h'
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub((X + Y) \times B)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X + Y
              is the least upper bound. This completes the proof.
          %p I think that’s enough for one day. I will continue to explore the categorical structure and logic of ETCS next time.
